
* User creates a tag in the git repository and pushes it to origin.
  * Tag must be named like any of these:
      v1.0.0
      v1.0.0-dev1
      v1.0.0-rc1
  * This will trigger a build of the "Make Release" plan in Bamboo.

* Bamboo checks out the repository for the given tag or revision.
* Bamboo invokes the jobs/buildRelease.sh script.
* Script sets up python environment and runs the releasebuilder python module.
* Python module:
  * Reads config file that contains:
    * Details on the compilation environment.
    * A list of details on runtime environments.
  * Clones the repo on the current node
      * Bamboo caches the repository and then clones from that local repository.
      * Get the url to the local cached repository by running: git remote -v | head -n 1 | awk '{print $2}'
        * Result must begin with "file://", must be a dir and must be resolvable.
      * Clone the repository to another local directory with: git clone --no-local <url> <dest>
  * Starts an EC2 instance for the compilation environment.
  * Copies the cloned repository across to the compilation environment.
  * Invokes the script to build the installer.
  * Copies the installer from the compilation environment back to the original machine.

  * For each runtime environment:
    * Starts an EC2 instance for the target environment.
    * Copies across the installer.
    * Copies across the cluster config.
    * Runs the installer.
    * Starts SE on the target environment.
    * Runs any tests against the target environment.
    * Collects any test result files and copies them back to the original machine.
    * Stops SE on the target environment.
    * Collects any log files and copies them back to the original machine.
  * Writes out a JUnix result file (if necessary).
  * Remove the temporary cloned repository.

Style guide and (some) linting checks can be validated by running:
  pipenv run flake8 --ignore=E501
